WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.240
- [Interviewer] So, the first
one is, um, this is from--

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:02.730
- [Director] Wait, can
I just have a quick,

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.100
one of you just do a quick clap for me.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.470
(Tan and Bobby clap)

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:06.303
Great.

00:00:06.303 --> 00:00:07.136
- [Interviewer] Wicked.

00:00:07.194 --> 00:00:08.670
(bubbly orchestral music)

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.783
I have found Queer Eye to be one

00:00:10.783 --> 00:00:13.130
of my favorite shows of the year.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.970
It's got such a fantastic feel for outta.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.680
I think the guys really care about

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.450
the people who they are transforming.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.750
But it opens up such an
interesting discussion

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.710
about mental health, about
looking after yourself,

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.410
self-care, and relationships.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.332
So, to celebrate the second season,

00:00:29.332 --> 00:00:32.193
we were asked if we wanted
to interview the guys.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.130
Essentially like, the week leading up

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.050
to the interview was not
that great, personally.

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.670
It was just really hard to
kind of, get through it.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.415
Like, I'm such a big fan of the show,

00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:42.240
and I was really trying to be
pumped up for the interview,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.070
but I was feeling very, very anxious

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.993
about meeting them, and like,
screwing it up, essentially.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:51.713
We've got one from Matthew, who says,

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.770
this is more interior design level,

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.367
say, "I'm a huge Star
Wars fan, and right now,

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:00.317
"my room, my apartment looks
more like a 14 year old's

00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:03.110
"boy's room, rather than
an adult apartment."

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:04.230
So it started off pretty well.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.857
The guys were really nice, but I thought,

00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:08.477
"Okay, just power through these questions.

00:01:08.477 --> 00:01:09.740
"Power through these questions."

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.770
And then we got to the bit that

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:14.560
I was asking Karamo about self-care

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:17.250
and helping yourself,
and he was coming back

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.680
with some really meaningful advice

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.430
that actually related incredibly much

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.320
to my own personal situation I'd been

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.924
going through for the previous few weeks.

00:01:25.924 --> 00:01:29.653
And it really overly resonated with me.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:34.437
So then, I was like, "Oh, that
was a really good answer."

00:01:34.437 --> 00:01:36.440
- And if you get a no today, it doesn't

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.940
mean you'll get a no tomorrow.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.275
- I'm keeping a note of that. (laughs)

00:01:41.275 --> 00:01:42.690
It happened again later on.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.853
There was a question about relationships.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:49.370
This one is quite sad, but hopefully,

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:50.220
we can find some good things.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.871
It says, "How do you move on from someone

00:01:51.871 --> 00:01:55.047
"you love, who doesn't love you back?"

00:01:55.278 --> 00:01:56.111
- Ooh.

00:01:56.111 --> 00:01:58.080
- There was another
fantastic set of points made,

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.830
and it felt really
personal, even though it was

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.480
actually somebody else's problem.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.470
Then, I was like, oh my God, this is just

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.100
so deeply personal.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:08.917
That is another one I'm
going to be writing down.

00:02:08.917 --> 00:02:10.900
(they laugh)

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.347
'Cause it's just, you
know, it's just (groans).

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:18.167
But then, I inadvertently
blurted out something like,

00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:19.717
"I'm having a shit month."

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.582
These questions aren't
from me, by the way.

00:02:21.582 --> 00:02:22.552
(they laugh)

00:02:22.552 --> 00:02:24.170
- You really are going
through it, aren't you?

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:25.864
- [Scott] Yeah, I'm having
a really shit month.

00:02:25.864 --> 00:02:27.278
- [Bobby And Tan] Awww!

00:02:27.278 --> 00:02:28.878
- No, I'm not, I'm not, I'm not.

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:30.943
No, no, no, it's fine.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.990
At the end of the interview,
just when we were wrapping up,

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.777
they're all getting up,
and about to go, Karamo

00:02:35.777 --> 00:02:37.977
then came over and was
like, "Are you okay?"

00:02:38.485 --> 00:02:39.360
And I was like, "I'm fine."

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.920
You know, I thought I was okay.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:45.180
And then, out of nowhere,
they all initiated a hug,

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:46.029
and they all kind of went in.

00:02:46.029 --> 00:02:49.529
(group cheers around him)

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.370
And my God, it was like,
the most unexpected

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.380
flow of emotion I've
had in such a long time,

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.950
and I was a bit beside myself.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.270
And I didn't kind of know what to do.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:03.000
It was, I mean, it smelled brilliant.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.800
It was fantastic, but I was a
bit of a mess, to be honest.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.163
What Queer Eye is so
fantastic at is kind of,

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.200
opening that discussion
about mental health,

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.027
about being you know, caring
for people around you.

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:19.020
And the fact that it's
okay not to be okay.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.203
And I think for me, that was just,

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.513
had such a resonance, that I
just didn't necessarily expect.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.280
And I think that's

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.870
just was really quite powerful to me.

00:03:31.208 --> 00:03:34.708
(soft contemporary music)

